Azureglobe
==========

Round-trip latency between Azure datacenters, on an interactive globe.

Pick a region and see how fast it reaches every other Azure region: routes
are colored by latency (green < 100ms, yellow 100-200ms, red > 200ms) and
listed from fastest to slowest.


Run locally
-----------

    git clone https://github.com/erenworld/azureglobe.git
    cd azureglobe
    pnpm install
    pnpm dev


Live latency test
-----------------

The "Test from my browser" button measures the latency from the visitor's
browser to every Azure region: for each region it sends one warm-up HTTPS
request (DNS + TLS), then 4 timed requests to a tiny public file hosted in
that region, and keeps the median.

Those files live in one small storage account per region, created by a
script:

    brew install azure-cli   # once
    az login
    ./infra/deploy.sh        # creates the accounts, writes
                             # website/public/endpoints.json

Commit website/public/endpoints.json so the site knows where to send
requests. Regions your subscription can't use (restricted ones) are
skipped. Cost is a few cents a month: each account holds a 2-byte file,
and you only pay for reads.

To remove everything:

    ./infra/destroy.sh

The visitor's position on the globe comes from the browser's location
permission (rounded to ~10 km, never sent anywhere). Without it, "you" is
placed at the fastest region.


Region-to-region data
---------------------

Latencies are the P50 round-trip times published by Microsoft in "Azure
network round-trip latency statistics":

    https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/networking/azure-network-latency

They live in website/app/azure-data.ts.


